## Examples

One concrete line per rule, with real values. Concrete beats abstract — "a 12MB upload
shows 'Files must be under 10MB'" is worth more than "validates file size".

- <example>

## Assumptions

Answers that were filled in with a sensible default rather than actually decided — usually
because it wasn't worth stopping the conversation over, or it was too early to know.

These are **not** decisions. They're here so they stay visible and cheap to overturn; an
assumption that quietly hardens into a requirement is how features end up subtly wrong.

- <what was assumed> — <why that default> — _revisit when: <what would settle it>_

## Open questions

Things nobody can answer yet. Leave them here rather than guessing — a guess written as a
requirement is indistinguishable from a decision later.

- [ ] <question> — <who might know>

**Lots of questions means this isn't understood well enough to build yet.**
**Lots of rules means it's too big — split it into separate journeys.**

